WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.489 --> 00:00:02.446
- [Voiceover] Ketchup.

00:00:02.446 --> 00:00:04.558
So American, it's basically the red

00:00:04.558 --> 00:00:06.702
in red, white, and blue, right?

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:09.082
And that red clearly is from the tomatoes

00:00:09.082 --> 00:00:10.605
the ketchup is made from.

00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:12.026
- [Voiceover] Yum!

00:00:12.026 --> 00:00:14.383
- [Voiceover] But that
wasn't always the case.

00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:18.160
The first recorded recipe
for ketchup comes from China.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.983
And for more than a thousand years

00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:22.897
it wasn't even made with tomatoes.

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:24.944
It was made with fish guts.

00:00:24.944 --> 00:00:26.444
- [Voiceover] Eww!

00:00:32.714 --> 00:00:35.440
- [Voiceover] Fish intestines, 
bladder, and stomach

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.393
all mixed together with salt,

00:00:37.393 --> 00:00:41.468
then sealed and heated in the
hot summer sun for 20 days.

00:00:41.468 --> 00:00:43.750
That was the original ketchup.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.995
A fermented fish paste that
dates back to 6th century China.

00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:50.129
It was popular throughout Southeast Asia

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.184
and British and Dutch settlers
who arrived in the 1600s

00:00:53.184 --> 00:00:54.668
loved the stuff.

00:00:54.668 --> 00:00:56.886
Over time, they brought
ketchup home to Europe

00:00:56.886 --> 00:00:59.091
and added their own modifications

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:01.428
including beer, mushrooms, walnuts,

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.261
oysters, strawberries and peaches.

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.368
By the mid-1700s, English
ketchup was a mainstay

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:09.788
on British dinner tables

00:01:09.788 --> 00:01:11.409
and as colonists went west,

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:14.156
it soon made its way across the pond.

00:01:14.156 --> 00:01:16.162
That's where tomatoes come in.

00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:17.922
They're native to the Americas.

00:01:17.922 --> 00:01:21.304
And it's rumored Europeans once
believed they were poisonous

00:01:21.304 --> 00:01:23.721
but poisonous, they were not.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.917
In 1812, a Philadelphia
horticulturalist and scientist

00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:32.718
by the name of James Mease
introduced tomatoes into the mix.

00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:35.083
He published a tomato ketchup recipe

00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:38.518
that was the beginning
of a new crimson era.

00:01:38.518 --> 00:01:41.622
From there, many different
iterations were concocted

00:01:41.622 --> 00:01:43.563
and by the end of the 18th century,

00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:45.644
The New York Tribune called tomato ketchup

00:01:45.644 --> 00:01:47.858
America's national condiment

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:50.632
that was on every table in the land.

00:01:50.632 --> 00:01:52.804
Which brings us back to this.

00:01:52.804 --> 00:01:54.959
Tomato ketchup is here to stay.

00:01:54.959 --> 00:01:58.682
- And I, for one,
don't miss the fish guts.

00:01:58.682 --> 00:02:01.765
(lighthearted music)

